    "updated_at": "2022-02-15T05:52:05Z",
    "body": "Hm ... are you maybe under the impression that _other_ literally includes `[` etc? It doesn't: the regexps are \"starting points\" only. Each time a literal teletype terminal appears in the syntactic grammar, it \"creates\" a new \"anonymous\" terminal that doesn't match any of the \"named\" terminals. So for example, \"attribute\" is not an _identifier_ even though it matches the regexp used to initially define _identifier_. When `attribute` first appears in teletype, it effectively changes the definition of _identifier_ to \"original regexp but not 'attribute'\". The same process removes '[', '{', '(', ')', '}', ']', etc from _other_. (In fact if _other_  is matched at all, in practice it is a syntax error - it's just in theory that an extended attribute grammar _could_ consider it valid.)\r\n\r\nI have a feeling it's the missing piece that's leading to the conclusion that unbalanced matches are possible. I'm pretty sure Web IDL is the only language I've seen with this spooky-action-at-a-distance approach to defining its terminal symbols and I think it probably deserves a more prominent note; right now, figuring this out feels some sort of Web IDL grammar hazing ritual.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
